e  what exactly you're looking for um you  can change the background you can add  like a drop down  uh with the mouse over yeah i really  like the the black background and  nice um you can do some drop lines here  uh so it'll it'll kind of show where  you're at on the  the main curve there wow okay  yeah uh it's pretty cool  yeah and and then for different  functions it will it might highlight  different  kinds of properties right you know sine  is  is a typical curve that will show your  derivative and integral  um here's one that also has some things  like discontinuities or  uh limits or derivatives or asymptotes  and things like that so wow  so that's it seems like that uh that  curve analysis is a pretty comprehensive  function then it  does a lot of heavy lifting there that's  yeah that's very nice um and it's  again it's it's using the wolfram  function repository so  uh anyone has access to this particular  function  um whether you're in the wolf alpha  notebook edition or just in a normal  wolfram language notebook whether that's  on the cloud or  um in your mathematica environment  yeah and and in this case uh it's  interesting that uh  the the code almost looks exactly like  the  natural language query that you've got  up there it's uh  you know it's just a few small  differences so right  typically or pretty much the only  additional thing is  this second argument here which is just  specifying what is the independent  variable for this particular function  function x exactly right now  all right so uh what else what else do  we have here  um one of the things that i find most  interesting about the wolfman alpha  notebook edition  is again the ability to do uh kind of  sequential computations  um and here's one and i'm a little  afraid to evaluate because of the  the connectivity issues we're having  earlier um but this is a problem from a  computer math uh competition exam  um typically this is a  competition where people are using  different programming languages to  to kind of answer these really  complicated questions  um so in this case the question was  something along the lines of  uh what is the maximum value of this of  the determinant of this four by four  matrix  um on a given interval um  and typically like that that's that's  definitely too complicated of a thing  that you can ask  wolfram alpha in one line you know  but here uh we're able to do it and and  just three  kind of questions here um so the first  one we're  we're defining that that matrix um that  four by four matrix as  this variable m and then we're setting  f to be the determinant of m and again  all i'm doing is typing in what you  would  uh you know what you would say out loud  or what you're doing  in your head to do this problem and then  i just say you know max  max of f for x between these two uh  numbers and um in just a couple seconds  uh we're able to understand what you  meant convert it to wolfram language  syntax and then spit out the answer here  right so so not only can you um sort of  chain  commands one to the next uh you can sort  of  keep things in memory the way that you  would with uh  well with programming or if you're using  a calculator for instance  uh so it seems like there's there's a  lot of  sort of the same functionality that you  might want from  you know any calculator or math tool all  sort of built in but  in a very intuitive way so you can just  say it the way that you would normally  say it yeah  exactly um i feel the wolfram language  already has a pretty low barrier of  entry  like the function that that computes the  maximum  value of a of a expression is called  maximize  um but sometimes like the ordering of  the syntax and when you need brackets  and things like that is a little  confusing and ambiguous especially to  someone who's new to programming  i think the wolf from alpha notebook  edition completely removes the  that even that small barrier of entry  that there is to  um to perform some really high level  computation  yeah yeah and it's it's pretty  impressive the things that you can get  just from that that sort of simple  workflow  um so is there anything else uh  in wolfram alpha notebook edition that  might be new  to some people that they haven't seen  before  yeah um one of the things that uh has  been a big influence  in uh  important addition uh in the last year  um  has been uh the expansion of our  scientific content um so i'm just the  the math manager like  uh i we kind of stick in number land on  my team but  um we work uh pretty collaboratively  with  the the other science departments and  the step-by-step for chemistry has  really come a long way in the last year  there's a recent blog post series by by  jason sonnenberg  who's the the primary step-by-step  developer and  uh and a lot of that stuff is uh let's  just say on its way to the wolfram alpha  notebook edition as well  um you can already do queries for for  various chemical properties and things  and  uh kind of hooking in the the step right  the step-by-step  for those as well like we have for math  content um  right should be here in in the next week  or two so cool  yeah yeah so it'll be useful in  chemistry class as well  yeah absolutely solving solving chemical  equations  instead of uh just mathematical ones all  right  cool so lots of interesting new features  um  so aside from just the function  repository resources  what other kinds of things are available  through that resource system  in in the notebook edition sure uh one  of the other  uh wolfram technologies that we've made  use of is the  the very popular wolfram demonstrations  project  so uh if i could share again to access  this in the wolfmelford notebook edition  it's as easy as asking for  uh demonstrations on a particular  subject so here i've  i've typed in uh derivatives  demonstrations and one of the  suggestions you get is an entity list of  that  click on that and then you get this  whole swath of things you can click on  wow yeah um i just clicked on this  graphs of taylor polynomials one  so this is something you know kind of  computes the the various taylor  polynomial of a function  um you can change the degree so you can  get more or less  curves here uh and then changing the  domain and range on this  this graph and see the different  behavior uh  pretty neat and again just from a single  query that that's what i was able to  get so yeah yeah and like i said with  the other one this this interactive  stuff just seems  really useful for anyone trying to  actually learn these concepts because  i definitely remember getting pretty  confused about taylor polynomials  and uh calc ii so yeah definitely  yeah so again one more uh really neat  thing that the wolf alpha notebook  edition can do that  that we can't quite do on the website  itself so  um you know we have some interactive  elements on the website but  nothing compares to what you can do  within a notebook right yeah  awesome well thank you for uh for  showing me some of this stuff that's new  and will from alpha notebook edition  um of course got new math new  step-by-step  interactive stuff resource access so  hopefully people are enjoying using it  and uh  definitely recommend any any students or  teachers out there  trying to figure out a good way to run  you know math courses or  even just work on a math homework the  wolfram alpha notebook edition  yeah for sure thanks so much thanks for  joining us  you can check out other wolfram tech  talks live coding streams and more  on the wolfram research youtube channel  or see what else is new at wolfram.com

